  "metadata": {
    "title": "SpaceX's $1.8 Trillion IPO Could Test Wall Street's Timing Playbook",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.\nWall Street is staring at a rare IPO setup. History says the biggest technology listings often stumble after the opening excitement fades, but SpaceX, Anthropic and OpenAI could be different because their scale is already massive. Truist Wealth reviewed 30 major technology IPOs over the past 15 years and found they averaged a maximum decline of 55% in the first year of trading, with six-month forward returns also skewing negative. That track record matters. SpaceX is seeking $75 billion in what is set to be the largest IPO of all time, valuing the company at about $1.8 trillion. Anthropic has raised private funding at a $965 billion valuation, while OpenAI is worth $852 billion based on its own fundraising.\nThe timing question is where investors need to be careful. These offerings could quickly capture both benchmark weightings and retail attention, especially as the market remains fixated on AI. Spending from Alphabet (NASDAQ:GOOGL), Meta Platforms (NASDAQ:META), Amazon.com (NASDAQ:AMZN) and Microsoft (NASDAQ:MSFT) has already helped lift revenues and share prices across industries tied to semiconductors, energy providers and construction firms. SpaceX, Anthropic and OpenAI are already viewed as leaders in this field, and their public listings could possibly deepen that position. But buying on day one may not be the only way to win. Matt Stucky of Northwestern Mutual Wealth Management noted that investors did not need to buy Google or Facebook at IPO to make money over the long term, suggesting that waiting six months or a year could still leave room for attractive returns.\nThe lesson from earlier megacap listings is mixed, not simple. Meta Platforms, then known as Facebook, priced its 2012 IPO at $38, opened around $42, closed basically flat, then fell more than 30% over the next 12 months before eventually rising more than 1,400% from its IPO price. Tesla (NASDAQ:TSLA) rose 18% after 12 months from its June 2010 debut, but still underperformed the S&P 500 and traded below its record high at the time before later gaining more than 25,000% from IPO. CoreWeave (NASDAQ:CRWV) has climbed more than 150% since its March 2025 IPO, but the path has been volatile: the offering was downsized, Nvidia stepped in to anchor the sale, the shares opened below the $40 IPO price, surged more than 400% to a June 2025 record, then later lost half that gain and remains down 44% from its peak. For investors, the takeaway could be that these IPOs may become historic, but the first entry point may not necessarily be the best one.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.",
      "Wall Street is staring at a rare IPO setup. History says the biggest technology listings often stumble after the opening excitement fades, but SpaceX, Anthropic and OpenAI could be different because their scale is already massive. Truist Wealth reviewed 30 major technology IPOs over the past 15 years and found they averaged a maximum decline of 55% in the first year of trading, with six-month forward returns also skewing negative. That track record matters. SpaceX is seeking $75 billion in what is set to be the largest IPO of all time, valuing the company at about $1.8 trillion. Anthropic has raised private funding at a $965 billion valuation, while OpenAI is worth $852 billion based on its own fundraising.",
      "The timing question is where investors need to be careful. These offerings could quickly capture both benchmark weightings and retail attention, especially as the market remains fixated on AI. Spending from Alphabet (NASDAQ:GOOGL), Meta Platforms (NASDAQ:META), Amazon.com (NASDAQ:AMZN) and Microsoft (NASDAQ:MSFT) has already helped lift revenues and share prices across industries tied to semiconductors, energy providers and construction firms. SpaceX, Anthropic and OpenAI are already viewed as leaders in this field, and their public listings could possibly deepen that position. But buying on day one may not be the only way to win. Matt Stucky of Northwestern Mutual Wealth Management noted that investors did not need to buy Google or Facebook at IPO to make money over the long term, suggesting that waiting six months or a year could still leave room for attractive returns.",
      "The lesson from earlier megacap listings is mixed, not simple. Meta Platforms, then known as Facebook, priced its 2012 IPO at $38, opened around $42, closed basically flat, then fell more than 30% over the next 12 months before eventually rising more than 1,400% from its IPO price. Tesla (NASDAQ:TSLA) rose 18% after 12 months from its June 2010 debut, but still underperformed the S&P 500 and traded below its record high at the time before later gaining more than 25,000% from IPO. CoreWeave (NASDAQ:CRWV) has climbed more than 150% since its March 2025 IPO, but the path has been volatile: the offering was downsized, Nvidia stepped in to anchor the sale, the shares opened below the $40 IPO price, surged more than 400% to a June 2025 record, then later lost half that gain and remains down 44% from its peak. For investors, the takeaway could be that these IPOs may become historic, but the first entry point may not necessarily be the best one."
    ],
